I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then identify candidate phases from the chemical constraints and peak positions. I’ll refine the strongest structural model(s) in GSAS-II, validate phase fractions and residuals, and export the requested CIFs, profile, JSON, report, and final project.

The chemistry points first toward strontium tungstates, but the phase count is explicitly unknown, so I’m treating that only as a search constraint. I’m now extracting peak positions/intensities and checking the installed crystallographic backends and reference database before choosing any structural model.

The dominant phase is now unambiguous: tetragonal scheelite SrWO₄ (I4₁/a). Its diagnostic reflections align across the full range, including the strong 112/103 overlap near 28°, 200 near 33.4°, 204 near 45.5°, and 312 near 56.1°. Several reproducible peaks remain outside that model, so I’m separating those residuals and testing precursor/product-side candidates rather than forcing a single-phase fit.

I’ve separated two securely identified components: scheelite SrWO₄ and monoclinic WO₃ (the latter is supported by its characteristic 23–25° triplet and matching higher-angle multiplets). I’m beginning quantitative GSAS-II trials now; I’ll use residual reduction and physically sensible refined cells/scales to decide whether any third phase is warranted.

The first quantitative trials confirm that WO₃ improves the fit, but its unconstrained crystallite-size parameter tried to compensate for still-unmodeled peaks, so I rejected that overfit. I’ve fixed the profile treatment and am comparing explicit third-phase candidates on equal terms; the key discriminator is the strong residual sequence at 29.76°, 32.61°, 44.67°, 48.61°, and 67.03°.

The three-phase model is now chemically and positionally coherent. I’m doing the last stability check—testing only minimal preferred-orientation and peak-profile corrections—before freezing the phase fractions and exporting the final GSAS-II project.

The orientation check found no meaningful texture in scheelite SrWO₄, but the minor monoclinic component is sensitive to how its overlapping intensities are modeled. I’m treating that as a real uncertainty rather than accepting a lower residual blindly, and I’m rechecking all chemically plausible Sr–W–O database entries against the unmatched peak positions.

The phase-position evidence still favors scheelite SrWO₄ + monoclinic WO₃ + a fergusonite-type SrWO₄ component. The current check is specifically separating true phase intensity from an uncalibrated instrumental-width effect; this is the main source of uncertainty in the minor-phase fraction.

The database-wide residual search confirms the third peak family is best matched by the fergusonite-type SrWO₄ entry; the next chemically plausible Sr–W–O candidate is substantially poorer. I’m now freezing a conservative model with no arbitrary texture correction, using a reflection-geometry sample-displacement correction, and exporting all requested deliverables from that same final project.

Identified three modeled crystalline phases:

- Scheelite SrWO₄ (I4₁/a): 76.95 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/n): 11.00 wt%
- Fergusonite-type SrWO₄ (I2/a): 12.06 wt%, provisional assignment

Fit: Rwp 16.47%, Rp 12.25%, GoF 4.66. The fergusonite-type model matches a distinct residual peak family but is lower confidence because its reference structure is pressure-stabilized and no pressure treatment was reported.

